Step 1: Open WSL

Open your Ubuntu WSL terminal.

Check that Ansible is installed:

ansible --version

You should see something similar to:

ansible [core ...]

If Ansible is not installed, install it first.

Step 2: Create a lab directory

Create a separate directory for this lab:

mkdir ansible-vault-lab
cd ansible-vault-lab

Check your current location:

pwd

You should be inside something similar to:

/home/your-user/ansible-vault-lab

Step 3: Create the group_vars directory

Run:

mkdir -p group_vars/all

Check the structure:

tree

If tree is not installed:

sudo apt install tree -y

Then:

tree

At this point you should have:

ansible-vault-lab/
└── group_vars/
    └── all/

Step 4: Create the Vault password

Now create a random password:

openssl rand -base64 2048 > vault.pass

Check that the file exists:

ls -l

You should see:

vault.pass
group_vars

You can check that it contains data:
wc -c vault.pass

You don't need to display the password on the screen.

Important

vault.pass is the password Ansible will use to encrypt and decrypt your Vault.

Do not upload this file to GitHub.

Step 5: Create the encrypted Vault file

Run:

ansible-vault create group_vars/all/pass.yml --vault-password-file vault.pass

An editor will open.

If it opens in nano, enter:

aws_access_key: YOUR_AWS_ACCESS_KEY
aws_secret_key: YOUR_AWS_SECRET_KEY

For example:

aws_access_key: AKIAxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
aws_secret_key: xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

Do not use these example values.

Use your own AWS credentials if this is your lab account.

Step 6: Save the file

If you are using nano:

Press:

Ctrl + O

Then press:

Enter

Then:

Ctrl + X

Ansible will save the file in encrypted format.

Instead, you should see something like:

$ANSIBLE_VAULT;1.1;AES256
323762643963...
...

This confirms that the file is encrypted.

Step 8: View the decrypted content

If you want to see the actual credentials again, use:

You should see:

aws_access_key: YOUR_AWS_ACCESS_KEY
aws_secret_key: YOUR_AWS_SECRET_KEY

Here Ansible is:

Encrypted pass.yml
       ↓
vault.pass
       ↓
Ansible decrypts it
       ↓
You can see the variables

Step 9: Edit the Vault later

If you want to change the AWS credentials later, don't use:

nano group_vars/all/pass.yml

Instead use:

ansible-vault edit group_vars/all/pass.yml --vault-password-file vault.pass

Ansible will decrypt the file temporarily, allow you to edit it, and encrypt it again when you save.

Step 10 — Add vault.pass to .gitignore

At this point, your directory should look like:

ansible-vault-lab/
├── vault.pass
└── group_vars/
    └── all/
        └── pass.yml

We want Git to ignore vault.pass.

a) Check that you are in the correct directory

Run:

pwd

You should get something like:

/home/bilal/ansible-vault-lab

Then:

ls

You should see:

group_vars
vault.pass

If you see these, you are in the correct directory.

b) Create .gitignore

Run:

touch .gitignore

This creates an empty file named:

.gitignore

Check:

ls -la

You should now see:

.gitignore
group_vars
vault.pass

c) Open .gitignore

Run:

nano .gitignore

An empty editor will open.

Type exactly:

Add:

vault.pass
group_vars/all/pass.yml

Save and exit.

That's it.

You are telling Git:

"Whenever you see a file named vault.pass, ignore it."

d) Save the file

If you are using nano:

Press:

Ctrl + O

Nano will show something like:

File Name to Write: .gitignore

Press:

Enter

Then exit:

Ctrl + X

e) Verify .gitignore

Run:

cat .gitignore

You should get:

vault.pass

So now your structure is:

ansible-vault-lab/
├── .gitignore
├── vault.pass
└── group_vars/
    └── all/
        └── pass.yml

f) Initialize Git

If this is a new Git project, run:

git init

You should see something similar to:

Initialized empty Git repository...

For your Ansible Vault lab, you probably want:

vault.pass
group_vars/all/pass.yml

to be ignored because they contain sensitive information.

Step 1 — Put these in .gitignore

Open it:

nano .gitignore

Add:

vault.pass
group_vars/all/pass.yml

Save and exit.

Step 2 — Check status
git status

You should now see something similar to:

Untracked files:
    .gitignore

Notice that:

vault.pass
group_vars/all/pass.yml

are no longer shown.

That's exactly what you want.

Step 3 — Add .gitignore
git add .gitignore

Then:

git status

You should see:

Changes to be committed:

    new file: .gitignore

_______________

Important concept

Think of it this way:

.gitignore
   |
   |-- tells Git what NOT to track
   |
   +---- vault.pass       ← ignored
   |
   +---- pass.yml         ← ignored

But .gitignore itself should normally be committed because other people cloning your repository also need to know which files should be ignored.

____________

i) Commit

Now commit your lab:

git commit -m "Add Ansible Vault setup"

Your encrypted:

group_vars/all/pass.yml

can be committed, while:

vault.pass

is ignored.

Step 12: Test the Vault password

You can verify that your password file works:

ansible-vault view group_vars/all/pass.yml --vault-password-file vault.pass

If the correct credentials appear, your Vault setup is working.

___________

Your Ansible Vault is working correctly

You ran:

ansible-vault view group_vars/all/pass.yml --vault-password-file vault.pass

and Ansible displayed:

aws_access_key: ...
aws_secret_key: ...

That means:

The file is encrypted, and your vault.pass password successfully decrypted it. ✅

If you run:

cat group_vars/all/pass.yml

you should see encrypted content beginning with:

$ANSIBLE_VAULT;1.1;AES256

________________

Step 13: Create a temporary wrong password

Run:

openssl rand -base64 32 > wrong.pass

This creates another password file:

wrong.pass

So now you have:

ansible-vault-lab/
├── .gitignore
├── vault.pass
├── wrong.pass
└── group_vars/
    └── all/
        └── pass.yml

The important difference is:

vault.pass  → correct password
wrong.pass  → wrong password

=> Try to open the Vault using the wrong password

Run:

ansible-vault view group_vars/all/pass.yml --vault-password-file wrong.pass

Ansible will try to decrypt:

pass.yml

using:

wrong.pass

But pass.yml was encrypted using:

vault.pass

=> Therefore, the decryption should fail.

You may see an error similar to:

ERROR! Decryption failed (no vault secrets were found that could decryt)
